Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.220
Protesters are back on the streets in Hong
Kong

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.910
They are now angry about a proposed extradition
law

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:13.719
Many people fear this law could be used by
the Chinese government as a tool to prosecute

00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:15.720
political opponents.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:24.310
These protests, however might not change anything
So let’s back up and see how all these happened:

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:28.990
As a special administrative region inside
Chinese territory, Hong Kong has a different

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:33.740
legal system, and has long enjoyed freedom
of speech that other parts of China don’t

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:34.740
have.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:35.740
The protesters are now angry about a proposed
extradition law

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.410
If the law passes, political opponents could
be sent to China and face unfair trials

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:48.160
Many people fear this law could be used by
the Chinese government as a tool to prosecute

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.160
political opponents.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.100
If the law passes, they might face unfair
trials in China.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.830
we can't give up simply because the government
ignored our demand, because this is our home.

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:56.520
I can see that there are many Hong Kong people
still coming out today and I'm sure there

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.340
will be more of us coming out in the coming
couple of weeks/// to show our opposition

00:00:58.340 --> 00:00:59.470
to the extradition law."

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:02.570
So why is this law being pushed to a vote?

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.790
Because of Hong Kong Chief Executive Carrie
Lam.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:11.250
Lam might be the leader of Hong Kong, but
she was not democratically elected.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.790
To even get on the list of candidates, she
needed the approval of the Chinese gov.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:20.799
It’s the Chinese government that allowed
her to become the head of Hong Kong.

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:30.240
The Chief Executive is not elected by the
people, but by a committee of 1200 members.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.990
And to get on the list of candidates, she
needed the approval of the Chinese gov.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:40.000
Responding to the angry protesters, Lam denies
the Chinese government’s role in the latest

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.000
bill proposal
SOT

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.590
However, if Lam wants to stay in power, or
even secure a second term, she needs the approval

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.130
of the Chinese government.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:51.590
And China has already expressed eagerness
to pass it

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:54.500
the central government will continue to firmly
support the Hong Kong government in completing

00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:00.130
the amendment of the 'two ordinances’ [passing
the extradition law].

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:04.729
For the bill to become law, it still needs
to get more than half of lawmakers’ votes

00:02:04.729 --> 00:02:06.979
and it’s probably going to pass.

00:02:06.979 --> 00:02:10.360
Because currently, the majority of them have
a pro-China stance

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.879
But... there’s still a slim chance for a
surprise outcome.

00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:21.609
In 2003, a controversial national security
law sparked demonstrations of over 500,000

00:02:21.609 --> 00:02:25.310
people
This led to a pro-Beijing party withdrawing

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:28.760
its support, forcing the government to shelve
the bill

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.079
This, however, only happened once.

00:02:32.079 --> 00:02:35.480
Now, protesters hope that history will repeat
itself

